 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A-suffix parts conduct one way and are marked with a cathode band; CA-suffix parts are bidirectional and unmarked. Both share one row of figures per voltage, except that a bidirectional part at VR of 10 V or below has double the IR limit. The forward voltage and forward surge ratings (Fig. 6) belong to unidirectional parts alone."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulse rating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The 1500 W rating is for one 10/1000 µs pulse, rising to its peak in 10 µs and decaying to half the peak at 1 ms (Fig. 3). VC in the table is each part's maximum clamping voltage at its own IPP for that pulse. Fig. 1 plots the allowed peak power against pulse length: about 50 kW at 0.1 µs, falling to about 0.8 kW at 1000 µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Derating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Peak pulse power and current hold 100 % to Ta = 25 ℃, then fall in a straight line to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 2). Steady dissipation PM(AV) is 6.5 W at low temperature and falls in a straight line from about 70 ℃ to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Forward surge",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A unidirectional part carries 200 A for a single 8.3 ms half sine starting at TJ max; with repeated 60 Hz cycles the allowed peak drops to about 52 A at 100 cycles (Fig. 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and capacitance",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Typical IR stays under 5 µA for parts above 12 V. The table limit is 1 µA from SMCJ11A upward and rises to 800 µA for SMCJ5.0A and SMCJ6.0A. Junction capacitance falls as breakdown voltage rises and is lower under reverse bias than at zero bias: at zero bias a unidirectional part is about 10 nF at 5 V and about 250 pF at 400 V (Fig. 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and failure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Glass-passivated junction in a molding compound rated UL 94V-0; matte tin, lead-free plating; halogen free and RoHS compliant. The terminals stand 260 ℃ for 40 s during soldering. Whisker tests follow JEDEC JESD201A, tables 4a and 4c. Driven past its voltage or current rating, the part typically fails short."
  }
 ],
